  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:20:12.359Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nA corporation must satisfy specific conditions to claim a bad debt as a deductible expense, primarily that the debt must be considered worthless and must have been previously included in gross income.\n\n### Interpretation\n1. The debt must be a bona fide debt that was previously included in the corporation's gross income.\n2. The corporation must demonstrate that the debt is worthless, meaning there is no reasonable expectation of recovery.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that corporations must maintain adequate records to substantiate the existence of the debt and its worthlessness. This includes documentation of efforts made to collect the debt and any relevant communications with the debtor. Proper substantiation is crucial for the deduction to be accepted by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) during audits.",
